 # GPUIX
 
+Neoism's raster canvas keeps a native GPUI paint cache for unchanged display
+lists. Command, image-store, scale, bounds, and clip changes invalidate it;
+interactive overlays remain live. Canvas text remains uncached so per-frame
+automation text stays complete. Ordinary retained-tree mutations notify the
+root instead of forcing a window-wide cache reset.
+Changed canvas lists paint immediately without queuing a second root redraw;
+GPUI discards their previous cached scene during that uncached frame.
+Window titles are sent to the platform only when changed. Optional
+`NEOISM_GPUIX_TREE_PROFILE=1` logs aggregate tree-lock waits and root construction
+timings once per second; it excludes subsequent GPUI layout and painting.
+Consecutive, pairwise-disjoint canvas images share bounded GPUI paint layers.
+Overlaps and clip or primitive boundaries split groups, preserving paint order.
+
+The pinned Wayland backend preserves continuous-axis logical displacement
+without the upstream threefold multiplier. Discrete wheel notches retain their
+three-row policy; synthetic pixel events and X11 are unaffected.
+
+### Display frame wake (Neoism binding patch)
+
+`GpuixRenderer.setFrameWakeCallback(callback)` supports native display-frame
+wakes on Linux, Windows, FreeBSD, and macOS. Passing `null` clears the weak,
+coalesced callback and invalidates its pending registration.
+`requestFrameWake(): boolean` requests one callback through GPUI's
+`Window.on_next_frame`. Repeated requests coalesce until JavaScript receives
+the callback. It returns false without an active callback or after shutdown.
+The request does not dirty the scene, force a draw, or run a repeating timer.
+The callback is a platform frame opportunity, not a physical presentation
+timestamp. Animation owners should prepare mutations there and request another
+wake only while work remains. Multiplex runtime ticks with animation work
+because the renderer has one wake callback.
+Visible animating windows retain display cadence when keyboard focus moves away;
+there is no additional inactive-window 30 Hz throttle. Idle windows still park.
+Production hosts build with `--no-default-features` and verify
+`testSupportEnabled() === false`; GPUI's test-support mode bypasses its
+production frame scheduling even on platforms with only a test-renderer stub.
+
+On Wayland the pinned GPUI patch retains compositor-paced Mailbox presentation.
+`GPUI_WAYLAND_PRESENT_MODE=fifo` selects FIFO for pacing diagnostics. Retry
+timers use the current output mode rather than assuming a 60 Hz display.
+`GPUI_WAYLAND_PRESENTATION_TRACE=1` logs compositor presentation timestamps,
+refresh intervals and discarded buffers for opt-in pacing diagnostics. `getRenderedFrameSample()` publishes a coherent draw count
+and timestamp; this measures root draws, not physical presentation.
+`getPresentedFrameSample()` returns the compositor-confirmed count and timestamp
+on Wayland, or null before feedback/on unsupported platforms.
+On Linux, Windows and FreeBSD, `simulateScrollWheel(x, y, dx, dy, modifiers, false)`
+queues benchmark input without synchronously blocking JavaScript behind a draw.
+Omitting the wait argument retains synchronous automation behavior. A final
+`precise` argument (default true) selects pixel input; false selects mouse-wheel
+notches so slow-wheel benchmarks exercise the real line-based input path.
+
 **React and Solid** for [GPUI](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/tree/main/crates/gpui), Zed's GPU UI framework.
 
 Write a React or Solid tree in TypeScript. GPUIX paints it with Metal, DirectX, or Vulkan. No Electron. No web view.
